[2026-11-10 06:52:10] LLM suggestion: ## Analysis

### Pareto Front Assessment

Let me identify the non-dominated candidates (both objectives measured, good quality):

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Quality Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| 17p5-0add-5deg-550s-N2-1450s | 912.84 | 0.27 | Top-edge gap |
| 17p5-0add-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1) | 860.49 | 0.27 | Wrinkle |
| 19-0add-5deg-0s-N2-1500s (run4) | 890.19 | 0.29 | Two small wrinkles |
| 17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2-1250s (run2) | 960.52 | 0.27 | Clean membrane |
| 18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run3) | 385.22 | 0.41 | Minor wrinkles |
| 17p5-0add-15deg-200s-NoN2-1800s | 340.70 | 0.38 | Clean membrane |
| 17p5-0add-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run4) | 330.99 | 0.44 | Clean membrane |
| 19-0add-10deg-240s-NoN2-1600s (run2) | 312.52 | 0.38 | Minor wrinkles |

**Approximate Pareto front** (non-dominated in modulus vs. pore fraction):
- High modulus / lower pore fraction: ~960 bar / 0.27 (run2 of 17p5-20deg)
- Balanced: ~767 bar / 0.34 (19wt-5deg-0s), ~730 bar / 0.22 (19wt-5deg-60s — but pore fraction is low and high CV)
- High pore fraction / lower modulus: ~330 bar / 0.44 (17p5-19deg-run4), ~385 bar / 0.41 (18-15deg-600s-run3)

The **critical gap** in the Pareto front is the middle region: **modulus ~500–700 bar with pore fraction ~0.35–0.42**. No confirmed clean membrane occupies this space.

### Validation Status Check

- **17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2-1250s (run2)**: 960 bar / 0.27 — very high modulus but run3 gave only 385 bar / 0.33. The two independent runs are wildly inconsistent (960 vs 385 bar), so this candidate is **not validated** and the high value is unreliable.
- **17p5-0add-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1)**: 860 bar / 0.27 — run2 gave 554 bar / 0.29, run3 gave 519 bar / 0.36, run4 gave 331 bar / 0.44. Highly variable across runs; not a stable high-modulus point.
- **18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s**: runs 1–5 show modulus ranging 340–477 bar, pore fraction 0.36–0.41. This is a **reproducible high-pore-fraction** region but with low modulus.

### Gap to Fill

The Pareto front needs a candidate with **moderate-to-high modulus (~600–800 bar) AND moderate-to-high pore fraction (~0.35–0.40)**. 

Looking at what drives higher pore fraction: lower bath temperature (5°C), longer wait times, no nitrogen, lower polymer concentration, and warmer bath temperatures (15–20°C) in some cases. Higher modulus tends to come from higher polymer concentration (19–20 wt%) and cold bath (5°C).

The combination of **higher polymer wt (~19 wt%) + cold bath (5°C) + moderate wait time** has shown modulus ~767–890 bar but pore fraction only 0.29–0.34. 

The **18 wt% + bath 15°C + no nitrogen + long wait** gives high pore fraction (0.36–0.41) but low modulus (340–470 bar).

An **unexplored region**: **19 wt% + bath 5°C + longer coupon_to_bath_wait_time (~200–300s) + no nitrogen** — the longer wait and no N2 may allow more solvent evaporation/skin formation that could increase pore fraction while the higher polymer concentration maintains modulus. This combination is genuinely novel: the 19 wt% experiments have mostly used 0–60s wait times and nitrogen.

Alternatively, **19 wt% + bath 10°C + moderate wait** — bath_temp 10°C is intermediate and may yield a better modulus/pore-fraction balance than 15°C (which gives low modulus) or 5°C (which gives low pore fraction).

The condition **19wt%, bath 10°C, 90s wait, N2, mixing 65°C, speed 10** is genuinely unexplored (the 19wt%/10°C experiments used 240s wait and no N2). This is a modest, informative step into an under-sampled region.

```json
{
  "mixing_temp": 65,
  "bath_temp": 10,
  "pullcast_speed": 10,
  "nitrogen": true,
  "coupon_to_bath_wait_time": 90,
  "nips_bath_wait_time": 1800,
  "polymer_wt": 19.0,
  "additive_wt": 0.0
}
```

**Rationale:** This experiment targets the under-explored region of **19 wt% polymer + 10°C bath + 90s wait + N2**. The 19 wt% concentration has consistently produced higher modulus (~600–890 bar) compared to 17–17.5 wt%. The 10°C bath is intermediate — colder than 15°C (which yields high pore fraction but low modulus) and warmer than 5°C (which yields low pore fraction). The 90s wait with N2 is a standard condition well-characterized at 17–18 wt% but never tested at 19 wt%. This combination could plausibly yield a membrane in the **600–800 bar modulus / 0.33–0.38 pore fraction** range, filling the Pareto gap between the high-modulus/low-pore-fraction and low-modulus/high-pore-fraction extremes. The parameters are all within well-functioning ranges and the condition is genuinely novel in the dataset.

